  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use an adaptive multiplicative smoothing algorithm that progressively refines the step function representation. The algorithm combines global exploration with targeted frequency-domain analysis to iteratively improve solutions. The key distinction is leveraging the continuous Fourier transform properties to identify and suppress localized high-overlap regions. This approach addresses the limitations of the previous local search methods by focusing on the spectral representation of the function, effectively capturing the harmonic structure of optimal solutions. The main heuristic is that reducing high-frequency components in the transform correlates with lower maximum overlap values in the time domain.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe represent the step function h(x) using a sampling grid over [0,2] with samples per point n. The optimization targets:\n- Raw domain: vector h in [0,1]^N with sum(h)=N/2\n- Frequency domain: non-negative spectrum of the autocorrelation (1-h)\u2297h\nObjective: minimize max_lag_value of the auto-correlation product\n\nThe algorithm alternates between:\n1. Frequency-domain analysis of current solution\n2. Modulated smoothing of dominant frequency components\n3. Amplitude adjustment preserving sum constraint\n4. Time-domain adjustment via inverse transform\n\nEach iteration enforces:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Sum(h) = N/2\n- Non-negative autocorrelation spectrum\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with initial_h_values if provided, otherwise generate random feasible vector\n   - All vectors are clipped and adjusted to meet sum constraint\n\n2. Core optimization loop:\n   a) Compute autocorrelation via FFT\n   b) Identify peaks above threshold at each lag\n   c) Select key frequency components to dampen by multiplying their estimated magnitude by 0.8-0.9\n   d) Adjust superposition weights to maintain global shape\n   e) Apply inverse FFT, then resample to integer grid\n   f) Clip values and enforce sum preservation\n\nConstraints handling:\n- Feasibility restoration: use recursive attribute repair after FFT operations\n- Lower bounds: ensure no component falls below 0.1 unless intentionally smoothed\n- Upper bounds: cap modifications based on local amplitude thresholds\n\n3. Diminishing perturbation schedule:\n   - Initial smoothing interval: 500 evaluations\n   - Interval length reduces by half every 100 evaluations\n   - Minimum perturbation: 1% of initial amplitude\n\n4. Progressive complexity management:\n   - Base resolution: start with N=360 samples\n   - Success triggers resolution doubling up to N=720\n   - Termination when time exceeds budget or improvement plateau is detected\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - Verify sum(h) == expected (within 1e-6)\n   - Confirm 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n   - Compute autocorrelation in full mode and clip at each lag\n\n2. Objective computation:\n   - Use normalized FFT correlation to maximize relevance\n   - Max(lag) value must be a finite number\n   - Store verification trace for comparison\n\n3. Termination conditions:\n   - Time-based cutoff at 1100 seconds\n   - Non-improvement streak of 20 evaluations\n   - Natural plateau detection in spectra analysis\n\nThe construction maintains the property that sum(h)=N/2 through periodic repair and multimodal averaging. The smoothness of the Fourier-space transformation ensures that extreme local dips are balanced out without creating unrealistic features.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. Primary variation: adaptive frequency damping factor (tunable \u03b2)\n2. Secondary variation: frequency smoothing window size (\u03c4)\n3. Tertiary variation: aggressive local repair vs. conservative damping\n\nThese variations are conditionally triggered based on:\n- Stage of optimization: early exploration allows higher frequencies\n- Magnitude of overlap reduction\n\nComputation budget and robustness\n1. Timing allocation:\n   - Initial resolution (N=360): 75% of total budget\n   - Promotion (N=720): triggered after 350 evaluations, uses remaining 25%\n   - Final refinement: 5% of budget for clipping and repair\n\n2. Failure modes:\n   - FFT normalization failure: revert to direct correlation\n   - Numerical zero eigenvalues: use pseudospectral factorization\n\n3. Emergency fallback:\n   - When sum cannot be enforced, use multistep random repair with local normalization\n\n4. Global fallback structure:\n   - Initial_h_values preserved as core fallback\n   - Intermediate solutions repaired via projected gradient method",
